.\" Manual page for linkrd
.TH LINKRD 1 "2026-10-27" "linkrd 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linkrd \- USB ACM agent for bidirectional text clipboard synchronization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linkrd
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B linkrd
.BI install
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-autostart ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-start ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR runtime\-options ...]
.br
.B linkrd start
.br
.B linkrd stop
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR seconds ]
.br
.B linkrd pid
.br
.B linkrd status
.br
.B linkrd uninstall
.br
.B linkrd autostart enable
.RB [ \-\-
.IR runtime\-options ...]
.br
.B linkrd autostart disable
.br
.B linkrd autostart status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linkrd
synchronizes the plain-text clipboard of the current desktop user session with
a Radxa Linkr device over a USB CDC-ACM serial port.
Only UTF-8 plain text is synchronized, including empty text.
Clipboard change monitoring, periodic refresh, deduplication, loop prevention,
write acknowledgments, heartbeats, and USB reconnection are supported.
Images, files, and chunked transfers are not implemented.
.PP
The agent must run as the same desktop user whose clipboard it synchronizes.
It talks to the device only through a serial port; Linkr itself handles the
browser WebSocket connection.
On Linux, the serial port is selected by USB vendor and product ID
(default VID \fB1D6B\fR, PID \fB0104\fR) or by an explicit device path.
.PP
Without a subcommand,
.B linkrd
runs in the foreground, logging to stderr and to a rolling log file, and can be
stopped with Ctrl-C or by the
.B stop
command.
.SH OPTIONS
Options that take effect at run time may also be saved by
.B install
and
.BR "autostart enable" ,
after a
.B \-\-
separator.
.TP
.B \-\-port \fIdevice\fR
Use an explicit serial device, for example
.IR /dev/ttyACM0 ,
.IR /dev/cu.usbmodem0123456789 ,
or
.IR COM3 .
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-vid ,
.BR \-\-pid ,
and
.BR \-\-serial\-number .
.TP
.B \-\-vid \fIhex\fR
Override the USB vendor ID (default \fB1D6B\fR).
The value is 16-bit hexadecimal and may carry a
.B 0x
prefix; it requires
.BR \-\-pid .
.TP
.B \-\-pid \fIhex\fR
Override the USB product ID (default \fB0104\fR).
The value is 16-bit hexadecimal and may carry a
.B 0x
prefix; it requires
.BR \-\-vid .
.TP
.B \-\-serial\-number \fIserial\fR
Only select a device whose USB serial number matches
.IR serial .
Useful when several Linkr devices with the same VID and PID are attached.
.TP
.B \-\-baud \fIrate\fR
Conventional ACM line coding, default \fB115200\fR.
This is not the USB transfer speed limit; any positive value is accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-ports
List detected serial ports and exit.
This does not access the clipboard, so it also works over SSH.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level \fIlevel\fR
Log verbosity: \fBoff\fR, \fBerror\fR, \fBwarn\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBdebug\fR,
or \fBtrace\fR.
Default is \fBinfo\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-dir \fIdirectory\fR
Write log files to
.IR directory .
Default is
.RI $XDG_DATA_HOME /linkrd/logs
(or
.IR ~/.local/share/linkrd/logs ).
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-no\-file\-log .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-file\-log
Log only to stderr and do not create log files.
.TP
.B \-\-startup
Internal option used by login autostart and by
.BR start :
reload the arguments saved in
.IR installation.json
and retry desktop initialization until the graphical session is ready.
It must be used alone.
Distribution packages start linkrd through a systemd user unit instead of
using this option directly.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Start the installed executable in the background using the saved runtime
arguments.
The installation record must exist and the installed executable must still
match its recorded checksum.
.TP
.B stop
Ask the running instance of the current user to shut down gracefully and wait
for it to exit.
A pending request is not a forced termination.
.TP
.B stop \-\-timeout \fIseconds\fR
Maximum number of seconds to wait for shutdown, from 1 to 300.
Default is \fB10\fR.
.TP
.B pid
Print the PID of the current user's running instance.
Exits with a non-zero status when linkrd is not running.
.TP
.B status
Print whether linkrd is running and its PID.
This reports process state only, not desktop or ACM health.
.TP
.B install
Copy the currently running executable to the per-user program directory and
start it in the background.
On Linux and other Unix-like systems the default directory is
.IR ~/.local/bin ;
Windows uses
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\ePrograms\elinkrd
instead.
The installation record and saved arguments are stored under
.IR ~/.config/linkrd .
The installer also configures
.B PATH
for the current user shell; follow its output and open a new terminal.
.TP
.B install \-\-dir \fIdirectory\fR
Install into
.I directory
instead of the default per-user location.
The binary is always named
.IR linkrd .
.TP
.B install \-\-autostart
Also register a login autostart entry
.RI ( ~/.config/autostart/linkrd.desktop
on Linux) so that linkrd starts with the next desktop login.
.TP
.B install \-\-no\-start
Install without starting the background process now.
.TP
.B install \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\- \fIruntime\-options\fR
Save the given runtime options for later background starts and for the login
autostart entry.
Without a
.B \-\-
separator the previously saved arguments are preserved.
.TP
.B uninstall
Stop nothing by itself: run
.B stop
first.
Remove the installed executable, the login autostart entry, the
.B PATH
configuration, and the installation record.
Log files and the configuration directory are retained.
.TP
.B autostart enable
Register the login autostart entry for the installed executable.
Optional runtime options may follow a
.B \-\-
separator and replace the saved arguments.
.TP
.B autostart disable
Remove the login autostart entry.
A running process is not stopped.
.TP
.B autostart status
Report the installed executable path, whether it is present, whether autostart
is registered, and the saved runtime arguments.
This inspects the registration only, not desktop/OS overrides or process
health.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/
Per-user configuration directory
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /linkrd
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set).
It contains:
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/installation.json
Installation record written by
.BR install :
absolute executable path, its SHA-256 checksum, and the saved runtime
arguments.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/runtime.lock
Lock file held while linkrd is running or being started.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/runtime.pid
PID of the running instance.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/runtime.control.json
Instance identifier used to validate local
.B stop
requests.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/runtime.stop
Pending graceful shutdown request.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linkrd/management.lock
Serializes management commands.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/linkrd.desktop
XDG login autostart entry created by
.B install \-\-autostart
or
.BR "autostart enable" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/linkrd/logs/
Default log directory
.RI ( $XDG_DATA_HOME /linkrd/logs
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set).
The active file is
.IR linkrd.log ;
rotated files are kept as
.IR linkrd.log.1 " ... " linkrd.log.6 .
Rotation happens daily and when a file would exceed 10\ MiB.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/linkrd
Executable path used by distribution packages.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-linkrd.rules
Distribution udev rule granting the logged-in desktop user access to the
Linkr CDC-ACM device through a seat ACL, with
.BR GROUP="dialout" " MODE="0660
as a fallback.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linkrd.service
Distribution systemd user unit.
It is installed but not enabled; enable it once per user with
.BR "systemctl --user enable --now linkrd.service" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for
.IR ~/.config ;
defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory for
.IR ~/.local/share ;
defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colored stderr logging when set to a non-empty value.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start in the foreground and select the port explicitly:
.PP
.RS 4
.B linkrd \-\-port /dev/ttyACM0 \-\-log\-level debug
.RE
.PP
List serial ports without touching the clipboard:
.PP
.RS 4
.B linkrd \-\-list\-ports
.RE
.PP
Per-user installation with login autostart and saved runtime options
(this is what
.B install \-\-autostart
does; distribution packages must not use it):
.PP
.RS 4
.B linkrd install \-\-autostart \-\- \-\-port /dev/ttyACM0 \-\-log\-level debug
.RE
.PP
Check and stop the current user's instance:
.PP
.RS 4
.B linkrd status
.br
.B linkrd pid
.br
.B linkrd stop \-\-timeout 30
.RE
.PP
Enable the distribution systemd user unit once per desktop user:
.PP
.RS 4
.B systemctl \-\-user daemon-reload
.br
.B systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now linkrd.service
.RE
.SH NOTES
Do not mix a distribution package with
.BR "linkrd install" .
The
.B install
subcommand copies the executable into the user's home directory and writes a
login autostart entry that runs the copied binary with
.BR \-\-startup ;
package upgrades cannot update that private copy, so a stale binary can keep
running and conflict with the packaged
.IR /usr/bin/linkrd .
Distribution packages ship the binary, a udev rule, and a systemd user unit
under
.IR /usr ,
and leave enabling the unit to the user.
.PP
Access to
.I /dev/ttyACM*
is granted by the udev rule through
.B TAG+="uaccess"
(systemd-logind seat ACL), so membership in the
.B dialout
group is normally not required.
On systems without seat ACL support the rule falls back to group
.B dialout
with mode 0660; log out and back in after joining the group.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred; for
.B pid
this includes linkrd not running.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemd.exec (5),
.BR udev (7),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
The project documentation is available at
.UR https://github.com/radxa\-linkr/linkrd
the Linkr project page on GitHub
.UE .
